        Also fixed here, found while sizing the field: Home's inset subtracted a *logical* BAR_HEIGHT from a *physical* buffer height (app.rs redraw_home), so on a scaled output the field painted into the strip the bar covers.

        **2026-09-07, second revision — the user reported "the bottom left is a mess". It was two things, and both are fixed here.**

        *One: the bar surface was a black block.* The paragraph above that said "the narrow surface is what makes dropping the ground safe" was wrong, and the reasoning inverted. `softbuffer`'s Wayland backend attaches a `wl_shm::Format::Xrgb8888` buffer (softbuffer 0.4.8, `src/backends/wayland/buffer.rs:102,142`): **this surface has no alpha channel and can never be transparent.** `HostedWindow::prepare` never clears the buffer either. A strip that painted only its chips was therefore an opaque *black* rectangle 280x48 in the bottom-left corner of the screen, growing to 336/392/564 as windows opened, with the chips sitting on it — and full width, black, whenever a row opened or a window was maximized. Narrow does not mean invisible; it only meant the black block was small. `BarScreen::draw` now fills its whole extent with `design::ground()` — Home's own ground colour — before anything else, and `every_pixel_of_the_surface_is_painted` fails if any state ever leaves a pixel unwritten again. That is visually identical to transparency today, because nothing but Home is ever behind the bar: the compositor reserves BAR_HEIGHT along the bottom edge so no client reaches it, Home is placed at the full output, and Home paints a flat `ui.theme.background`.

        *Two: Home painted a second row of widget controls inside the bar's strip.* Measured off Lane 1's nested screenshot rather than guessed: three boxes at y 752-782 (the output is 800 tall and the field ends at 752), x 463-725, 732-994 and 1001-1262, each of them the bottom 31 pixels of a widget tile — eight rows of `shell_fill`, then the `shell_low` region strip carrying Lock/Log out, Clear and Open, then the tile's bottom edge stroke. They were tiles placed a row below the document's own grid, and `lege_field::cell_rect` **extrapolates rather than clamping**: `edge()` is linear in the cell index with no bound at `count`, so a placement at `row == rows` is handed a rectangle one row below `bounds`, which is exactly the bar's strip. `FieldScreen::tile_rect` now passes every rectangle through `nudge_inside`, which shoves it back inside `self.bounds` at full size; paint and pointer both go through that one function, so the rectangle a person aims at and the rectangle that reacts move together.

        That is a clamp at the paint, not a repair: the document should never hold a placement outside its own grid, which belongs to `lege_field`'s reflow (`set_grid` → `find_placement`) and not to this crate. Marked `ponytail:` in the code.

        *Also:* a chip with no icon took its letter from the first character of the entry's label, and a one-window entry is labelled with its window title — so a kitty sitting in a directory put a chip reading "/" on the bar. `chip_initial` takes the application's own short name first and falls back to the first *alphanumeric* character of the title.

        **Deferred, with its trigger and its route, so nobody re-derives it:** real per-pixel transparency needs an `Argb8888` surface instead of softbuffer's `Xrgb8888`. The cheap route is *not* a bridge-owned surface with wl_pointer routing — winit already owns the bar's `wl_surface` and already delivers its pointer events. It is: an alpha-preserving variant of `previews::ShmBuffer::write` (~10 lines, the existing `Argb8888` path `session_lock::paint` already uses), a bridge `attach_argb` reaching the surface through the raw window handle the way `set_layer` does (~70 lines with a cached buffer and `set_buffer_scale`), and skipping `HostedWindow::present` for the bar (~20 lines), plus tests — about 150 lines. **The trigger is a wallpaper or any non-flat Home ground.** `shell.wallpaper.enabled` is in the settings schema (`lege-session-core/src/settings.rs:136`) and nothing implements it; the day something does, the opaque ground stops matching what is behind it and this becomes required. Note also that pixelkit colours are `0x00RRGGBB` with no alpha, so alpha would have to be reconstructed by keying on the ground colour — which leaves antialiased chip edges opaque and ground-coloured.

        One thing deliberately *not* changed: `wanted_size` stays computed after the paint in `redraw_bar`. `WindowEvent::Resized` already re-requests a redraw, so the one frame in which a chip is clipped by an out-of-date surface size self-corrects; moving the call before the paint would compute `row_open()` from the previous frame, and a row opened by the hover deadline would then get no room until some later redraw.
        """
    acceptance {
        check a-chip-never-wears-punctuation {
            statement """
                A chip with no icon takes its letter from the application's own name, falling back to the first alphanumeric character of the title — never the leading slash of a path.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-shell a_chip_takes_its_letter_from_the_application_and_never_from_punctuation"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.field-stays-above-the-bar-strip-20260907/1 ]
        }
        check bar-interaction {
            statement """
                A one-window cell activates; a multi-window cell opens its row and a row item activates; hover opens after the dwell and closes after the grace; controls exist only for a maximized activated window; the network indicator runs its declared action.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-shell bar_screen"
            verified_by [
                @legeos.evidence.bar-ground-and-pcmanfm-role-20260907/1,
                @legeos.evidence.bottom-bar-tags-app-groups-gate-20260907/1,
                @legeos.evidence.home-chips-projects-appearance-lock-20260907/1
            ]
        }
        check gate-green {
            statement """
                just check passes, including check-actions (network.open-control keeps a pointer route) and the re-reviewed Home baseline hash.
                """
            method command
            command "just check"
        }
        check hands-on-confirmation {
            statement """
                At the desktop: with nothing open the bottom of the screen shows only the network and clock chips, not a band across the screen, not a black block in the corner and no second row of tile controls beside them; each open application is one chip, groups first; hovering an application with two windows opens a row above the bar; a maximized window's park/maximize/close sit at the bar's right and act.
                """
            method manual
        }
        check no-bar-with-nothing-on-it {
            statement """
                The bar is the chip strip's own width normally, and the full output width only for a maximized window's controls or an open hover row; wanted_size is pure and the host sizes the surface to it.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-shell wanted_size_is_the_chips_alone_until_a_row_or_controls_need_the_width"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.home-chips-projects-appearance-lock-20260907/1 ]
        }
        check the-bar-is-not-a-black-block {
            statement """
                The bar surface writes every one of its own pixels in every state, so a surface that cannot be transparent (softbuffer attaches Xrgb8888) never shows as black behind the chips; the resting strip is chips on Home's own ground.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-shell every_pixel_of_the_surface_is_painted"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.bar-ground-and-pcmanfm-role-20260907/1 ]
        }
        check the-strip-holds-only-the-bar {
            statement """
                Home paints nothing in the bottom BAR_HEIGHT of the output: a tile placed outside the document's own grid is drawn back inside the field rather than one row below it, where cell_rect's extrapolation put it.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-shell the_field_paints_nothing_in_the_bar_strip"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.field-stays-above-the-bar-strip-20260907/1 ]
        }
    }
    implements [ @legeos.decision.one-persistent-bottom-bar-tags-not-bands ]
    part_of [ @legeos.work.home-surface-usability-next ]
    supersedes [ @legeos.work.bottom-bar-taskbar/4 ]
}

record legeos.work.bottom-bar-taskbar/6 : work {
    title "Lane B — shell: the persistent bottom bar with indicators, the taskbar with its hover row, and the maximized window's controls"
    state active
    scope [
        path "compositor/lege-comp/src/backend/mod.rs",
        path "compositor/lege-comp/src/xdg_shell.rs",
        path "crates/lege-window/src/geometry.rs",
        path "interfaces/wayland/lege-shell-v1.xml",
        path "shell/lege-shell/**"
    ]
    intent """
        shell/lege-shell: taskbar.rs (pure entries: window groups first in group-id order, then one entry per application through dock::group ByApplication; a one-window application is that window; active marks the activated toplevel) and surface/bar_screen.rs (BarScreen with one layout read by both paint and hit-test). app.rs hosts it as a third winit window on Layer::ShellOverlay with a bottom exclusive zone of BAR_HEIGHT (48), created after the bridge connects, dropped while a window is fullscreen, pointer-only; Home insets its field by BAR_HEIGHT; a BridgeState generation counter redraws Home and the bar the moment the window list changes; hover deadlines join the control-flow chain.
        """
    note """
        Behaviour ported from cosmic-app-list (GPL-3, libcosmic); no code copied. The compositor registers an exclusive zone against the first output only, so a second monitor gets no bar (compositor ceiling, follow-up). The compositor edits need a logout; the bar itself is hot-swappable.

        **2026-09-07, revised to the user's own decision:** "I do not want there to be a taskbar visible if there is nothing to show on it. Should just be tabs for the time/date/network status, like the tabs for windows. It can become a full row when there is something more to show."

        The bar is now a strip of chips — CHIP_H = lege_window::geometry::TAG_HEIGHT, on the compositor's own name-tag colours (design::TAG_FILL / TAG_INK, copied per contract K3, not shared — the shell does not link the compositor) — and the surface is exactly as wide as the chips are. There is no full-width shell ground at rest. Two things claim the width: a maximized window's controls, which belong at the right-hand end of the screen rather than beside the clock, and an open hover row, which needs somewhere to open into. `bar_screen::wanted_size(entries, activated, row_open, full_width)` is the single pure answer to which of those is true, and app.rs sizes the surface to it.

        This rests on the K1 zone contract: the compositor no longer stretches a zone-holding surface to the output, so it also never configures the bar to the output width. The shell supplies that number from Home's own configured logical width — Home is Layer::HomeSurface with no zone, so it is still placed at the full output, and it is in the same process. The exclusive zone stays BAR_HEIGHT along the whole bottom edge whatever the surface's own extent is, so toggling between chips and full width moves no window.

        Also fixed here, found while sizing the field: Home's inset subtracted a *logical* BAR_HEIGHT from a *physical* buffer height (app.rs redraw_home), so on a scaled output the field painted into the strip the bar covers.

        **2026-09-07, second revision — the user reported "the bottom left is a mess". It was one failure in two places, and both are fixed here: a surface that paints only part of itself keeps whatever was there before, because nothing clears these buffers (`HostedWindow::prepare` deliberately does not) and softbuffer's Wayland backend attaches `wl_shm::Format::Xrgb8888`, so there is no alpha to fall back on either.**

        *One: the bar surface was a black block.* The paragraph above that said "the narrow surface is what makes dropping the ground safe" was wrong, and the reasoning inverted (softbuffer 0.4.8, `src/backends/wayland/buffer.rs:102,142`). A strip that painted only its chips was an opaque *black* rectangle 280x48 in the bottom-left corner, growing to 336/392/564 as windows opened — and full width, black, whenever a row opened or a window was maximized. Narrow does not mean invisible; it only meant the block was small. `BarScreen::draw` now fills its whole extent with `design::ground()` — Home's own ground colour — before anything else, and `every_pixel_of_the_surface_is_painted` fails if any state leaves a pixel unwritten again. That is visually identical to transparency today, because nothing but Home is ever behind the bar.

        *Two: a row of widget controls sat inside the bar's strip, under the clock.* Measured off Lane 1's nested screenshot: three boxes at y 752-782 on an 800-tall output whose field ends at 752, each the bottom of a widget tile — body fill, then the `shell_low` region strip carrying Lock/Log out, Clear and Open, then the tile's bottom edge stroke. **They were stale pixels, not a stray placement.** `app.rs::redraw_home` insets the drawn area by BAR_HEIGHT only while `self.bar.is_some()`, and the bar is created after the bridge connects; the shell therefore draws several frames at the full height of the output, with tiles laid out down to the bottom edge. `FieldScreen::draw` then filled only `area`, Home's buffer is never cleared and its size never changes — so from the frame the bar appeared, nothing ever wrote to those last 48 rows again and they kept the bottoms of the taller frame's tiles for the rest of the session. `ui.painter.clear(ui.theme.background)` replaces the partial fill. The test draws one taller frame and then one inset frame into the same buffer; without the fix it reports 24606 stale pixels, the first at (377, 800) in `0x00d5dae0` — the tile edge colour measured at the box borders in the screenshot.

        The document was never at fault, which is worth writing down because it is the obvious suspect: every `FieldOp` that places a tile goes through `FieldDocument::is_free`, which checks `Placement::fits`; `set_grid` reflows its misfits and leaves an unplaceable one unplaced; and `find_placement`, `nearest_free` and `yielded_placement` are all bounded by the grid. Replaying the real stored layout (29x17 with the five default tiles) through `SetGrid` to a 14x8 grid leaves every placement fitting. `FieldScreen::tile_rect` still passes every rectangle through `nudge_inside` as a **defence**, because `lege_field::cell_rect` extrapolates rather than clamping — `edge()` is linear in the cell index with no bound at `count` — so an out-of-grid placement, if one ever appeared, would be drawn on top of the bar.

        *Also fixed here, found while tracing the `HomeField1.Apply refused: no such tile` warnings in the live journal:* `refresh_field` sent the grid resize and the merge as **two** fire-and-forget `spawn_field_apply` calls, two independent tasks on two D-Bus calls with no ordering between them. The resize has to land first — the merge's placements were chosen against the new grid — and when it did not, the daemon refused the batch while the shell kept the state it had already applied to itself, diverging the two documents for the rest of the session; every later sweep then names a tile the daemon never took, which is the run of warnings. It is now one ordered `Batch`, taken or refused whole, and the warn site names the op and its body.

        *Also:* a chip with no icon took its letter from the first character of the entry's label, and a one-window entry is labelled with its window title — so a kitty sitting in a directory put a chip reading "/" on the bar. `chip_initial` takes the application's own short name first and falls back to the first *alphanumeric* character of the title. And `redraw_toast` filled a rounded rect over its whole surface, leaving four black corners for the same reason; it clears first now.

        **Deferred, with its trigger and its route, so nobody re-derives it:** real per-pixel transparency needs an `Argb8888` surface instead of softbuffer's `Xrgb8888`. The cheap route is *not* a bridge-owned surface with wl_pointer routing — winit already owns the bar's `wl_surface` and already delivers its pointer events. It is: an alpha-preserving variant of `previews::ShmBuffer::write` (~10 lines, the existing `Argb8888` path `session_lock::paint` already uses), a bridge `attach_argb` reaching the surface through the raw window handle the way `set_layer` does (~70 lines with a cached buffer and `set_buffer_scale`), and skipping `HostedWindow::present` for the bar (~20 lines), plus tests — about 150 lines. **The trigger is a wallpaper or any non-flat Home ground.** `shell.wallpaper.enabled` is in the settings schema (`lege-session-core/src/settings.rs:136`) and nothing implements it; the day something does, the opaque ground stops matching what is behind it and this becomes required. Note also that pixelkit colours are `0x00RRGGBB` with no alpha, so alpha would have to be reconstructed by keying on the ground colour — which leaves antialiased chip edges opaque and ground-coloured.

        One thing deliberately *not* changed: `wanted_size` stays computed after the paint in `redraw_bar`. `WindowEvent::Resized` already re-requests a redraw, so the one frame in which a chip is clipped by an out-of-date surface size self-corrects; moving the call before the paint would compute `row_open()` from the previous frame, and a row opened by the hover deadline would then get no room until some later redraw.
        """
    acceptance {
        check a-chip-never-wears-punctuation {
            statement """
                A chip with no icon takes its letter from the application's own name, falling back to the first alphanumeric character of the title — never the leading slash of a path.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-shell a_chip_takes_its_letter_from_the_application_and_never_from_punctuation"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.field-stays-above-the-bar-strip-20260907/1 ]
        }
        check bar-interaction {
            statement """
                A one-window cell activates; a multi-window cell opens its row and a row item activates; hover opens after the dwell and closes after the grace; controls exist only for a maximized activated window; the network indicator runs its declared action.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-shell bar_screen"
            verified_by [
                @legeos.evidence.bar-ground-and-pcmanfm-role-20260907/1,
                @legeos.evidence.bottom-bar-tags-app-groups-gate-20260907/1,
                @legeos.evidence.home-chips-projects-appearance-lock-20260907/1
            ]
        }
        check gate-green {
            statement """
                just check passes, including check-actions (network.open-control keeps a pointer route) and the re-reviewed Home baseline hash.
                """
            method command
            command "just check"
        }
        check hands-on-confirmation {
            statement """
                At the desktop: with nothing open the bottom of the screen shows only the network and clock chips, not a band across the screen, not a black block in the corner and no second row of tile controls beside them; each open application is one chip, groups first; hovering an application with two windows opens a row above the bar; a maximized window's park/maximize/close sit at the bar's right and act. The journal shows no run of `HomeField1.Apply refused`.
                """
            method manual
        }
        check no-bar-with-nothing-on-it {
            statement """
                The bar is the chip strip's own width normally, and the full output width only for a maximized window's controls or an open hover row; wanted_size is pure and the host sizes the surface to it.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-shell wanted_size_is_the_chips_alone_until_a_row_or_controls_need_the_width"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.home-chips-projects-appearance-lock-20260907/1 ]
        }
        check off-grid-tiles-stay-in-the-field {
            statement """
                A placement outside the document's own grid is drawn back inside the field rather than one row below it, where cell_rect's extrapolation would put it.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-shell a_tile_placed_off_the_grid_is_drawn_back_inside_the_field"
            verified_by [
                @legeos.evidence.field-stays-above-the-bar-strip-20260907/1,
                @legeos.evidence.home-clears-the-bar-strip-20260907/1
            ]
        }
        check the-bar-is-not-a-black-block {
            statement """
                The bar surface writes every one of its own pixels in every state, so a surface that cannot be transparent (softbuffer attaches Xrgb8888) never shows as black behind the chips; the resting strip is chips on Home's own ground.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-shell every_pixel_of_the_surface_is_painted"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.bar-ground-and-pcmanfm-role-20260907/1 ]
        }
        check the-strip-holds-only-the-bar {
            statement """
                Home clears its whole buffer, so the bottom BAR_HEIGHT keeps nothing from the taller frames the shell draws before the bar exists.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-shell the_bar_strip_never_keeps_pixels_from_a_taller_frame"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.home-clears-the-bar-strip-20260907/1 ]
        }
    }
    implements [ @legeos.decision.one-persistent-bottom-bar-tags-not-bands ]
    part_of [ @legeos.work.home-surface-usability-next ]
    supersedes [ @legeos.work.bottom-bar-taskbar/5 ]
}

record legeos.work.build-set-archive-verification/1 : work {
    title "D2 — BuildSetId feeds debian/changelog, the local archive verifies whole sets, and the image can consume a published set"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "ci/**",
        path "cli/lege/src/dev/repo.rs",
        path "crates/lege-build/**",
        path "debian/changelog",
        path "debian/rules",
        path "distro/apt/**",
        path "tools/xtask/src/archive.rs",
        path "tools/xtask/src/build_set.rs",
        path "tools/xtask/src/signatures.rs"
    ]
    intent """
        Make the BuildSetId the one version authority (generate the debian/changelog head entry from it for source builds), add `lege dev repo verify` (one id across the set, no missing component against components.toml, valid InRelease/Release.gpg, previous set still installable), give the archive a staged/candidate marker distinct from the boot-deployment candidate vocabulary, record provenance (toolchain, host, Ubuntu index time), add a disposable Ubuntu 26.04 source-build lane under ci/ that reports not-executed when mmdebstrap/podman are absent, and provide the mkosi drop-in that points the image at a named published set.
        """
    note """
        HEAD reconciliation 2026-09-03: BuildSetId, keep-3 pruning, signing and mixed-set rejection already exist (crates/lege-build, cli/lege/src/dev/repo.rs); debian/changelog carries an independent 0.1.0~dev1; no verify command, no clean-root lane, no local-snapshot consumption by just image.

        2026-09-07: `image-drop-in` was already satisfied and nobody had said so. `distro/image/compose.sh:22` writes `mkosi.conf.d/40-local-archive.conf` from `$LEGEOS_BUILD_SET` or the archive's `current` marker, and writes nothing when neither exists (@legeos.observation.the-image-drop-in-for-a-named-build-set-already-exists). It has never been *run*, because mkosi is not installed on this host — which is the alternative the check's own wording allows, and is why its method is now observation rather than manual. `just image summary` is a user gate in RUNBOOK-L3.md.
        """
    acceptance {
        check archive-verify {
            statement """
                `lege dev repo verify` detects a mixed set, a missing component and a bad signature in fixtures, and reports the current and previous sets as installable.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege repo"
            verified_by [
                @legeos.evidence.archive-verification-checks-rerun-20260907/1,
                @legeos.evidence.de01-integrated-gate-20260904,
                @legeos.evidence.de01-integrated-gate-20260904/1
            ]
        }
        check image-drop-in {
            statement """
                A documented mkosi drop-in makes `just image` consume a named published set from the local archive, or the drop-in is documented as not exercised because mkosi is absent.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [
                @legeos.evidence.de01-image-summary-mkosi-absent-20260904,
                @legeos.evidence.image-drop-in-exists-unexercised-20260907/1,
                @legeos.observation.the-image-drop-in-for-a-named-build-set-already-exists
            ]
        }
        check one-version-authority {
            statement """
                The debian/changelog head entry used by the source build is derived from the BuildSetId; a test proves the derivation and `just deb-plan` prints the same id.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-build && just deb-plan"
            verified_by [
                @legeos.evidence.archive-verification-checks-rerun-20260907/1,
                @legeos.evidence.de01-integrated-gate-20260904,
                @legeos.evidence.de01-version-authority-20260904,
                @legeos.evidence.de01-version-authority-20260904/1
            ]
        }
    }
    implements [ @legeos.decision.debian-packaging ]
    part_of [
        @legeos.milestone.developer-edition-0-1,
        @legeos.work.deployment-activation-and-resourced
    ]
}

record legeos.work.compositor-window-authority/1 : work {
    title "The compositor's window authority: typed layers, one scene, explicit minimisation, recovery without the shell"
    state completed
    scope [ path "compositor/**", path "crates/lege-window/**", path "interfaces/wayland/**" ]
    intent """
        Make lege-window the thing that decides stacking and geometry rather than a set of helpers: typed overlay layers as a total order with rules about which client may claim which layer; the one-workspace invariant as a type-level property, with deterministic migration when an output is removed and validation of restored geometry against the current topology; explicit minimisation to a Window Shelf that is always restorable to a recoverable geometry; a compositor-owned recovery band, a show-all-windows scatter and an escape route out of fullscreen that all work when the shell is dead; drag anchors held in one global logical coordinate space across mixed-scale outputs; window commands gated on activation grants with recent-input evidence; and property tests over generated multi-output topologies. lege-comp-integration keeps the render thread off D-Bus, hardens the identity cache against PID reuse, and tells the governor which instance the user is looking at.
        """
    part_of [ @legeos.milestone.m1-session-runtime ]
}

record legeos.work.consumers-cli-portal-shell/1 : work {
    title "The clients: the CLI over the whole OS, one portal backend, the Home Surface model, Settings"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "apps/lege-settings/**",
        path "cli/lege/**",
        path "crates/lege-client/src/sdk.rs",
        path "portals/**",
        path "shell/**"
    ]
    intent """
        Make the services reachable: lege as one command line over identity, launching, associations with an explanation, files and operations, resources, history with undo, providers, settings, projects, power and updates; xdg-desktop-portal-lege as the single backend implementing FileChooser over the shared last-directory answer, OpenURI and AppChooser over the association broker, Settings and Background over providers and autostart, so a sandboxed application and a normal one behave the same; the Home Surface as a data model over the services (drawers, dock grouping by canonical AppId, the window shelf, the operations drawer) rendered without a toolkit for now; and lege-settings as pages that map one-to-one onto D-Bus interfaces, with nothing a private implementation detail. Everything the shell shows must be available through lege as well.
        """
    after [
        @legeos.work.compositor-window-authority,
        @legeos.work.governor-containment,
        @legeos.work.host-update-transaction,
        @legeos.work.sessiond-persistence-and-launch
    ]
    part_of [ @legeos.milestone.m1-session-runtime ]
}

record legeos.work.deployment-activation-and-resourced/1 : work {
    title "WS6 — build identity, local archive, lege dev deploy, compatibility gates, and replay-gated resourced improvements"
    state active
    scope [
        path "cli/lege/**",
        path "crates/lege-build/**",
        path "crates/lege-field/**",
        path "crates/lege-state/**",
        path "debian/**",
        path "distro/**",
        path "docs/adr/**",
        path "interfaces/**",
        path "justfile",
        path "scripts/reinstall-legeos-desktop.sh",
        path "services/lege-boot-health/**",
        path "services/lege-hostd/**",
        path "services/lege-resourced/**",
        path "session/units/**",
        path "tools/xtask/**"
    ]
    intent """
        Tracks A-E, landing order A1 A2 A3 A4 B1 B2 B3 E0 C1 C2 B4 E1 E5 E6 D1 D2 E2 E3 E4 C3. Track A: BuildSetId is the Debian version (0.1.0~dev<epoch>+g<sha7>[.dirty]) across all 13 packages via cargo xtask build-set, which rejects mixed sets; every long-lived process writes a runtime build stamp file at startup (filesystem-first because comp and shell serve no D-Bus; not RuntimeDirectory=, which would delete siblings' stamps); distro/deploy/components.toml is the machine-readable component matrix; lege dev status computes installed-versus-running convergence; doctor gains BuildSetCoherent and RunningMatchesInstalled with PackageAgesConsistent as the documented fallback. Track B: a local signed APT repo at /var/lib/legeos/devrepo (apt-ftparchive + per-developer gpg key, keep=3 sets), one transactional apt-get install --allow-downgrades per set, lege dev rollback as the same path, and an activation engine with per-component rules — shell restart with health gate, apps at next launch, resourced controlled restart, sessiond/filed/portal only when the compatibility gates pass (shipped manual until the composed-session restart test exists), hostd blocked mid-transaction, compositor always needs-session, kernel/boot needs-reboot — ending in the convergence report with the previous set retained. Track C: xtask interface-baseline/check-interface-signatures turns the frozen-signature convention into an enforced gate; lege-state gains schema_meta with min_reader_version, an open()-time refusal of too-new databases, VACUUM INTO snapshots, and the documented one-rollback-generation policy; lege-field gains a document migration registry applied at the load boundary with DocumentTooNew never overwriting. Track D: lege-boot-health calls MarkGood/Rollback itself, and hostd's reconcile_boot wires the GRUB tries counter; cryptographic verification and the hardware matrix stay explicitly deferred. Track E, all gated behind the E0 replay harness (replay recorded traces through Governor::decide with a round-trip fidelity test): thresholds scale with MemTotal (reserve clamp 6.25% 768MiB-4GiB, critical clamp 2% 256MiB-1.5GiB, reproducing today's constants at 48GiB), unsaved-work gains a producer and a TTL via additive SetInstanceWork, richer safe-to-sacrifice signals via additive SetInstanceActivity (idle age, project importance, checkpoint freshness reaching scoring for the first time, user pin as strong weight never hard exclusion), Governor::plan returns multi-step reclaim plans (Elevated trims up to three, Severe throttles top-K to the deficit, Critical stays single-victim) with the foreground structurally excluded at Severe unless demonstrably the runaway, protect.rs stops lowering memory.low and covers sessiond and filed, and ADR-0014 settles systemd-oomd ownership (armed backstop on app.slice at 90%, off on session.slice, avoid on the five session units).
        """
    note """
        Verified corrections folded in: MSRV is 1.97 workspace-wide per ADR-0008 (debian/control, packaging/README.md and CLAUDE.md prose are stale and get the three-line fix); protect.rs currently lowers protection every tick; has_unsaved_work has no producer at ObserveOnly; the health-to-promotion loop is open on both boot backends. Terminate stays unreachable and no phase raises a resourced stage; hand-authored fixture traces prove the harness and properties, not real-world calibration. Settled: per-developer signing key framed as built-on-this-machine; Build1 interface deferred; user pins persisted in sessiond; foreground-runaway thresholds are declared guesses; greeter/DM matrix row ships manual and disabled.
        """
    acceptance {
        check convergence-report {
            statement """
                lege dev deploy ends with a per-component report (build set, needs-session, needs-reboot), a schema-compatibility verdict, and the previous set named for rollback; lege dev status agrees with the stamps.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo run -q -p lege -- dev deploy --dry-run && cargo run -q -p lege -- dev status"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.de01-convergence-and-protect-20260907 ]
        }
        check protect-never-lowers {
            statement """
                protect.rs writes memory.low only when the desired value exceeds the current one, and covers lege-sessiond and lege-filed.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-resourced protect"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.de01-convergence-and-protect-20260907 ]
        }
        check replay-fidelity {
            statement """
                Replaying a recorded trace with the config that recorded it reproduces its recorded decisions, and every E1-E4 change quotes its replay diff against the fixture traces.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-resourced replay"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.de01-replay-and-signature-gates-20260907 ]
        }
        check rollback-acceptance {
            statement """
                With lege-shell deliberately broken, the set is rejected or health-gated before logout, and lege dev rollback restores the previous set without rebuilding.
                """
            method manual
        }
        check signature-gate {
            statement """
                check-interface-signatures passes on the committed baseline and fails when a shipped argument type is edited in place.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo run -q -p xtask -- check-interface-signatures"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.de01-replay-and-signature-gates-20260907 ]
        }
    }
    derived_from [ @legeos.assessment.hotfixability-current-state-20260831 ]
    part_of [ @legeos.work.design-consolidation-roadmap ]
}

record legeos.work.deployment-rollback-completion/1 : work {
    title "D5 — persisted deployment identities, bounded retention, snapshot before userspace mutation, and one diagnostic naming every set and deployment"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "cli/lege/src/dev/**",
        path "cli/lege/src/main.rs",
        path "distro/deploy/components.toml",
        path "services/lege-boot-health/**",
        path "services/lege-hostd/src/**",
        path "services/lege-hostd/tests/**"
    ]
    intent """
        Assign and persist previous/current/known-good deployment identities; add restart tests for Trial→Promoted/RolledBack; replace the fabricated zero deployment size with a real estimate and bounded retention that never drops the known-good or booted deployment; classify disk exhaustion during Staging; snapshot the root subvolume before a userspace apt install when on Btrfs and show it in `lege dev deploy --dry-run`; add a broken-sessiond fixture to the activation gate; and one status section naming installed set, running set, rollback set, root snapshot, booted deployment and known-good deployment.
        """
    note """
        HEAD reconciliation 2026-09-03: the phase machine, journal and resume are tested for Downloading/Staging/Verifying/AwaitingReboot; userspace and boot rollback are already separate paths; previous_deployment is declared but never assigned; retention trims with a fabricated 0 size.
        """
    acceptance {
        check dry-run-names-everything {
            statement """
                `lege dev deploy --dry-run` and the status section name the installed, running and rollback sets, the root snapshot, the booted and the known-good deployment; the live rollback, logout and reboot remain user-run.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege dev::"
            verified_by [
                @legeos.evidence.de01-deploy-dry-run-20260904,
                @legeos.evidence.de01-deploy-dry-run-20260904/1,
                @legeos.evidence.de01-integrated-gate-20260904
            ]
        }
        check identities-persist {
            statement """
                previous, current and known-good deployment identities survive a hostd restart and a resume from the Trial phase promotes or rolls back correctly.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-hostd"
            verified_by [
                @legeos.evidence.de01-integrated-gate-20260904,
                @legeos.evidence.de01-integrated-gate-20260904/1
            ]
        }
        check retention-and-disk {
            statement """
                Retention keeps N deployments but never the known-good or booted one, and disk exhaustion during Staging is a classified failure with the previous set named.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-hostd storage"
            verified_by [
                @legeos.evidence.de01-integrated-gate-20260904,
                @legeos.evidence.de01-integrated-gate-20260904/1
            ]
        }
    }
    implements [
        @legeos.decision.boot-deploy-secure-boot,
        @legeos.decision.one-privileged-host-daemon
    ]
    part_of [
        @legeos.milestone.developer-edition-0-1,
        @legeos.work.deployment-activation-and-resourced
    ]
}

record legeos.work.design-consolidation-roadmap/1 : work {
    title "Integrated design roadmap — Home, Files, perimeter resize, event services and Developer Edition storage"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "apps/lege-files/**",
        path "cli/lege/**",
        path "compositor/lege-comp/**",
        path "crates/lege-appd/**",
        path "crates/lege-associationd/**",
        path "crates/lege-field/**",
        path "crates/lege-file-index/**",
        path "crates/lege-session-core/**",
        path "crates/lege-state/**",
        path "crates/lege-types/**",
        path "crates/lege-window/**",
        path "debian/**",
        path "distro/**",
        path "installer/**",
        path "interfaces/**",
        path "services/**",
        path "shell/lege-shell/**",
        path "tools/xtask/**"
    ]
    intent """
        Execute the reconciled design in seven phases. P0 migrates durable claims and document references into AKR. P1 adds deterministic two-axis resizing from every side half and real diagonal cursor presentation. P2 establishes recoverable state-and-event service contracts and audits authorities, activation and bounded timer exceptions. P3 makes Home persistent project intent, native Files the folder surface, the taskbar the ordinary window selector, and Enter/Launch Tool/Restore distinct provider-backed actions. P4 adds a reviewed, identity-safe provisioning plan with explicit create/reuse/format choices and optional independent data roles. P5 connects that storage backend to the existing live/recovery and Developer Edition lanes. P6 adopts build placement, index checkpoints, richer developer status and presentation refinements only after native Lubuntu measurements justify them.
        """
    note """
        Sequence P0 first. P1 and P2 may then proceed independently. P3 depends on its own authority/subscription contracts, not completion of the entire P2 audit. P4 follows the current D1 package and D2 archive contracts; P5 follows P4 plus current D3/D5 work. Existing Developer Edition, distribution, index, activation and Home evidence remains valid and must not be restarted. The active native Files and bottom-bar decisions override older PCManFM-default, taskbar-free-chrome and automatic-shelf prose. Existing-partition mode currently reformats both ESP and root; ChangesSince uses an event sequence; DRM named cursors currently render as an arrow.
        """
    acceptance {
        check document-concordance {
            statement """
                The remaining architecture, ADR, protocol, research, measurement, planning and testing documents have an explicit authority/retention classification, and retired root-draft references are migrated.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [
                @legeos.evidence.legacy-root-doc-migration-20260907/1,
                @legeos.evidence.remaining-doc-authority-audit-20260907/1
            ]
        }
        check knowledge-migration {
            statement """
                Every adopted contract, rejected alternative, deferred experiment and unfinished acceptance gate from the seven root drafts is understandable from AKR without relying on those Markdown files.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [
                @legeos.evidence.astra-consolidation-advice-20260907/1,
                @legeos.evidence.legacy-root-doc-migration-20260907/1
            ]
        }
        check native-validation {
            statement """
                All implementation phases use native Lubuntu for build, test, packaging, runtime, hardware and deployment evidence; WSL is never cited as LegeOS validation.
                """
            method manual
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.native-lubuntu-validation-20260907/1 ]
        }
    }
    depends_on [ @legeos.constraint.native-lubuntu-is-the-development-environment/1 ]
    derived_from [ @legeos.assessment.hotfixability-current-state-20260831 ]
    implements [
        @legeos.decision.event-driven-service-authorities,
        @legeos.decision.home-projects-and-tool-actions,
        @legeos.decision.perimeter-resize-selects-two-axes-by-side-half,
        @legeos.decision.representation-ladder-is-the-organizing-principle
    ]
    part_of [ @legeos.work.home-field ]
    source {
        kind external
        role origin
        document "design-review-home-and-platform-20260901"
        start_byte 0
        end_byte 28861
        start_line 1
        end_line 579
        excerpt_hash "sha256:63db09f6fe91f5b42ff5e6ad53665baebc3271a567f94b9a81d33bd6ee92bbcf"
        use """
            Preserves the original six-workstream consolidation review.
            """
    }
